# 24. Andaman Island for the Socialist!
Tamil title: 24. அந்தமான் தீவு, சமதர்மிக்கு

“The fellow speaks of an ideal, does he? An ideal! Send him to Siberia!” the Tsar commanded when he saw people who wished to wage a moral struggle so the poor might live.

Siberia was a warehouse of death, a frozen desert filled with tortures—a place of bitter cold and ice fields stretching as far as the eye could see. Before the revolution, many were driven there merely for arguing that the poor too deserved a life.

“What did that villain say? That the luxurious lives and arrogance of the rich must end? What insolence! Send that arrogant fellow to the Bastille!” Before the French Revolution, the king issued such orders.

The Bastille was a terrifying prison, a subterranean vault that destroyed the bodies of those who entered it inch by inch and killed them.

“Does he oppose imperialism? Is he learning to throw bombs? Very well, send him to the Andaman Islands!” British imperial fanatics commanded in this fashion.

The Andamans were an uncivilised enclosure in the middle of the sea, an abode of malaria, islands capable of turning human beings into animals.

Siberia, the Bastille and the Andamans differ in size, arrangement and name, but as systems all three are one. Autocrats subject those who demand rights to cruelty; that is their method. Every country has its chamber of cruelty, and every place gives it another name.

Siberia embodied the Russian tyrant's cruelty; the Bastille was the French ruler's poisonous fang; and the Andamans were the man-killing institution British imperialism built for governing the Indian territory.

Only after enduring cruelties such as Siberia, the Bastille and the Andamans could the world break the systems of Tsar, autocratic monarch and imperial frenzy and advance by one step.

The ruler who drove revolutionary heroes to Siberia was one day hunted like a rabid dog by an enraged people. The Bastille was reduced to dust. Even the Andamans are now discussed not as a prison enclosure but as a place to be made prosperous.

Yet today, someone speaks openly as the representative of yesterday's arrogant rulers, without fear that the people will become angry or shame that sensible people will regard him with contempt.

“Send them to the Andaman Islands!” he says. Lacking authority to command, this honourable man can only suggest that they be sent. Had he obtained an opportunity to rule, he would already have issued the order.

Whom does he wish to send there? Those who say the poor must have life, deprivation must end and the fragrance of socialism must spread.

Because people have advanced somewhat intellectually, it is impossible to declare this ideal wrong or unnecessary. This great man therefore cannot perform that service. But he neither wishes to remain quiet nor to permit a scheme capable of disturbing the comfortable lives of the selfish.

Instead of openly condemning socialism and demanding that socialists receive life sentences in the Andamans, this crow has chosen a more elegant mode of speech.

“Send them to the Andaman Islands. Let them go there and work according to their splendid scheme,” he said in a mocking tone.

Autocratic servants with a trace of humour speak similarly when arresting someone:

“They say you roam outside and ruin your health. His Majesty is deeply distressed. Determined that your health must be protected, he sent me with this small force to perform the task properly. From now on you must remain safely and without anxiety in the mansion the king has selected for you. From this minute you are a guest of the government. Here is the firm token of that honour.”

Then they arrogantly place irons upon the prisoner's hands.

Rather than roar with that same arrogance, “Drive the socialists to the Andamans,” the speaker adopted an amusing manner—like pouring sweet milk into a golden cup, mixing in two drops of deadly poison and asking a smiling woman capable of enchanting anyone to serve it.

India, he argued, is too large a place to test whether the socialist ideal is practicable. The government should send socialists to the Andamans, provide every facility and let them conduct their experiment there. If they succeed, we may observe it and then understand what is right.

A prominent man uttered these words while living under Congress rule and Nehru's government, while the ears of this country's youth had not become deaf, while the fragrance of the ideals they had studied and heard had not faded, and while their idealistic dreams had not dissolved.

Young people were still planning in their hearts to turn the country into a flower garden and give the people a new life. Yet the speaker was neither an imperial servant, a French king nor a Russian Tsar. He was D. C. Driver, president of the Indian Chamber of Commerce in Calcutta, who spoke on 27 February and said socialists should be sent to the Andamans.

Do not imagine this worthy man referred only to the socialists gathered under Comrade Jayaprakash Narayan.

His intention embraces anyone who says the present capitalist system must go; factories must become the common property of the people; instruments of production must not be hunting grounds; and the system of profit proportional to cunning, arrogance proportional to wealth and government influence proportional to arrogance must yield to work according to ability and facilities according to need.

All such people, in his view, should become prisoners of the Andamans. The proper place for people who speak socialism is the island once designed for life prisoners to suffer.

Let them go there, listen to the surrounding sea, fight malaria, level mounds, turn forests into fields, control wild streams, build and organise a town, and make the place a field where their ideal can operate successfully.

Meanwhile, relieved of the trouble caused by socialists who speak of practices unknown in the town, millionaires can remain peacefully in India. Using workers who know only how to labour, they can accumulate production; use foreign agents to discover convenient places for hunting profit; advance into all such places; heap up profits; and appoint millionaires as agents.

They may advise rulers, donate generously to temple guardians for ablutions and worship, and hire religious orators at prices suited to their trade in words to lecture workers about the world's impermanence and the philosophy of sin and merit.

They may commission a thousand elegant *venba* verses comparing the state of a handsome man's heart when he meets at twilight, beside a hill of beauty, a maiden whose cheeks and sidelong eyes are red—with the state produced when snakes engage in love play inside an anthill—and arrange for their ceremonial presentation.

They may also commission a painting in which a maiden enters a pond; the lotuses gather and quarrel with her; she gives them gold and jewels; they say these are insufficient and demand her sari; they seize and pull it while the embarrassed and angry maiden pulls back her garment.

Capitalists may live thus in the Indian subcontinent; socialists must live in the Andaman Islands.

Had anyone spoken this way before 15 August, every newspaper in the country would have spat fire. They would have carried the headline “Roar of an Imperial Agent!” and published countless cartoons.

They would have passed resolutions fiercely condemning the arrogance of a man who said socialism was a noble ideal but its adherents should be sent to the Andamans. Hartals and strikes would have marked the protest. Events would have ranged from student slogans to an adjournment motion in the provincial legislature.

What now? It is like a thief stung by a scorpion: not a single voice of condemnation. Driver spoke publicly, yet no one in the country even answers his view.

Consider how much courage—a new courage—must have been born in a capitalist for him to say socialism is not practicable in this country and may be tested upon some island.

Leave aside what gave him such looseness of tongue and courage of mind. What silenced the mouths of these revolutionary heroes?

They entered the British imperial lion's cave and seized and shook its mane. Yet when capitalism enters their very bedroom and says with arrogant mockery, “The sky is your roof and the mound your flower bed; go outside, brother,” what did they do? What could they do?

Shame coated them so thickly that they could not even allow their anger to appear. Driver understood the astonishing change in their condition; that is why he could say, “Send them to the Andamans.” As a businessman, would he not know the state of the market?

He learned that arrogant goods were in great demand at this moment and therefore spoke arrogantly. The fault is not his. The condition has arisen in which the greatest heroes have had their ribs broken.

When even the Allahabad Pandit who once spoke socialism comes forward in Delhi's Parliament to answer the Tata-Birla group with affection and support, what surprise is there that others' minds have grown numb and capitalist conceit reached its summit?

An enemy attacks a warrior who has lost his sword. Here, capitalism mocks those whose determination and attachment to principle have collapsed. It asks, “Do you know your place, brother?” and graciously advises the rulers: “Send these people to the Andamans.”

We have heard many people criticise and condemn socialism in the past. Leaders would immediately boil up in protest; if they forgot, the youth would rise. They would say capitalists spoke so boldly because British imperialism supported them. Capitalists truly did remain restrained and fearful.

Those observing recent times closely can see capitalists and their agents now openly condemning and mocking socialist doctrine, with great arrogance mixed into their speech.

The manner of explanation and petition has vanished. Condemnation, mockery, challenge, threat and opposition have increased. Anyone who calmly reads the speeches periodically made by capitalist agents can understand this.

The age when people said, “Socialism is a fine ideal, but we can consider it only after national liberation,” has passed.

The age when they roared, “Only socialist life can abolish the poverty of this country's millions, and imperialism must disappear if such a life is to be obtained,” has passed.

An age has arisen when they mock: “Some people merely talk of socialism, some bookish philosophy; they do not understand the country's condition.”

An age has arisen when rulers reassure capitalists: “We shall not enter a frenzy and uproot capitalism at once merely because socialists cry out.”

An age has arisen when capitalists threaten the government itself: “Some people unnecessarily accuse capitalism. The government must allow no room for this. Talk of state ownership and socialist plans will damage industrial growth; capitalists will not come forward to invest.”

Now an age has arisen in which capitalists dare, even if “in jest,” to advise the government to send socialists to the Andamans.

Were the Dandi march, baton charges, destruction of the Rowlatt Act, Lajpat Rai's death, resistance to the foreigner, Bhagat Singh's hanging, the filling of prisons, removal of the Neill statue, satyagraha and Kumaran's death—all these heroic deeds—ultimately meant to create Drivers who say those advocating socialism as a programme fit to give the poor life should be sent to the Andamans?

We ask the young people who performed heroic service. We ask thoughtful people to reflect. We ask all the radicals now turned in different directions: was all that struggle undertaken to witness this condition?

The source then gives Driver's proposal at length:

> Bundle up your marvellous ideals and put them away. Give the capitalist system at least twenty years to live as it does now. Factories should bear only taxes at the level that existed before the war, and there should be no policy of controlling them.
>
> If capitalism is granted twenty years of freedom to act on its own without these restraints, we shall create an industrially advanced India equipped with every material facility required in war or peace.
>
> At a time when the factory system has only now begun to take root, it is unwise to uproot the old method and experiment with new ones.
>
> Rather than test ideals such as socialism throughout this vast subcontinent, let the government test the socialist programme in some specified place such as the Andaman Islands.
>
> Let the government give socialists every facility to go there and put their splendid programme and doctrine into practice. There will be no capitalists there to exploit them. If the socialists conduct their scheme and succeed, India can then observe it and understand what is good and bad.

These are the precious utterances contained in the capitalist Driver's speech.

Do not disturb the capitalist system.

Do not conduct the socialist experiment here.

This divine person states it plainly.

Their domination must remain unshaken for twenty years, while no socialist experiment may occur here.

The rich in India; socialists in the Andamans. Nehru must remain in Delhi to complete this “noble deed” properly. That is his thought.

Why should idealistic declarations such as “If one person has no food, we shall destroy the world,” “All are rulers of this country,” and “There must be a condition in which everyone has everything” be allowed here?

Why should people vainly believe that a path has opened for some great transformation in their lives?

Idealists to the Andamans; profit hunters to India. What a wondrous suggestion he has bestowed. What caused all this?

Congress came forward to govern, overestimated the strength of capitalism and became frightened. Capitalists secured places within Congress itself. The rulers developed a terror that the good government won for the country could not be preserved without capitalist cooperation and friendship.

Congress leaders therefore came forward to remove capitalism's suspicion, fear, agitation and sorrow, believing they must neither oppose it nor act in a way that displeased it:

“We shall not change the system suddenly.”

“Those who invest great capital in industries need not fear that their position will suddenly collapse.”

“The government will not attempt to take over and operate existing private industries.”

“It will conduct only certain specified new industries.”

“The government's entire purpose is merely to increase production.”

“If the capitalist method serves that end, the government will not interfere needlessly.”

Great Congress leaders continue speaking in this fashion.

Even petty princes could be made to withdraw by telegram. But before magnates such as Tata and Birla, they can speak only with fear mixed into affection. Such is their affection and fear towards those possessing economic domination.

If capitalism can produce so much fear at the outset while it is still a cub, what will happen if it receives the twenty years of dominion Driver demands, grows and the cub becomes a tiger? Will no intelligent person consider how much the fear will grow?

The Calcutta gentleman spoke in the confidence that people would not consider it. He demands a twenty-year lease upon the Indian subcontinent liberated through blood, tears and sweat.

Do you consent to leasing it away, allowing predatory profiteers' arrogance to grow and driving to the Andamans those who dare speak of radical doctrine and ideals?

Was heroic freedom obtained—not begged for, but fought for—to attain this condition?

The time has come for everyone committed to progressive policy and socialist ideals to remain extremely vigilant. Ideals have become objects of ridicule. Socialism is called an unreachable fantasy, and those who wish to reconstruct the country according to it are called dream wanderers.

The cry has arisen: India for the rich, the Andamans for the socialists.

Before it grows from speech into action, radicals must unite. Will they try?
